==============================================================================
(B) KR-type random order (non-manifold, the uniform-measure-DOMINANT family)
==============================================================================
  KR 3-layer order: layer sizes (150, 300, 150) (N=600), cross-layer prob p=1/2
    measured ordering fraction  r = 0.37404563
    'MM dimension' naively returned  d_MM = 2.3797653  <-- a spurious near-integer (the trap)
    (manifold range: f(2)=0.5, f(3)=0.228571, f(4)=0.1)
    height = 2 (a 3-element chain exists: True; a 4-chain is impossible with 3 layers)
    expected r from the layered combinatorics = 0.37562604 (matches measured)
    longest chain: 2d sprinkling (N=600) = 46 elements  vs  KR order = 3 elements (height 2, any N)
    => the height-based estimator SEPARATES them even though d_MM ~ 2.2 does not (the MM trap).

==============================================================================
(C) the dominance problem -- non-manifold (KR) orders dominate the uniform measure on posets
==============================================================================
  n=  100:  KR leading log2(#posets) ~ n^2/4 = 2500.0 bits
  n= 1000:  KR leading log2(#posets) ~ n^2/4 = 250000.0 bits
  n=10000:  KR leading log2(#posets) ~ n^2/4 = 25000000.0 bits
  n=10000:  manifold-like embedding data ~ n log2 n * (coords) = 531508.5 bits  <<  KR 25000000.0 bits
  => manifoldlikeness needs a non-uniform measure (a dynamics / selection rule): the open core.

